[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: dns_server
diagnosis_text:
This is not a routing/OSPF failure. IP reachability to the campus DNS server is healthy, but the DNS service on the server itself is down. From host_1_1_1_1, ping to 10.200.0.2 (dns_server) succeeds with 0% loss (~2 ms), and /etc/resolv.conf correctly points to nameserver 10.200.0.2. However, DNS lookups fail with “connection refused” when querying 10.200.0.2:53 (nslookup and dig both report connections refused), indicating the host is reachable but nothing is listening on the DNS port.

On dns_server, there is no listener on port 53 (ss/netstat show only 127.0.0.11 services; no :53), no DNS daemon processes are running, and systemd reports named.service is enabled but Active: failed (failed). This explains the symptom: applications attempt to resolve via 10.200.0.2, but the kernel returns RST/ICMP port-unreachable because named isn’t bound to UDP/TCP 53, so name resolution fails even though routing works.

Localization: dns_server, DNS subsystem (BIND/named), port 53. Root cause: the DNS daemon (named/BIND) on dns_server has failed and is not listening on port 53.

Corrective fix: restart the DNS service on dns_server (systemctl restart named), ensure it is enabled (systemctl enable named), and verify listeners on :53 (ss -lntu | grep ':53'). No changes or restarts are needed on the OSPF core; the path is operational.
=== END ===